Let $V$ litre of $10 \mathrm{~N} \mathrm{HCI}$ be mixed with $(1-V)$ litre of 4 $\mathrm{N} \mathrm{HCl}$ to give $(V+1-V)=1 \mathrm{~L}$ of $7 \mathrm{~N} \mathrm{HCl}$.

\begin{aligned}
N_{1} V_{1}+N_{2} V_{2} &=N V \\
10 V+4(1-V) &=7 \times 1 \\
10 V+4-4 V &=7 \\
6 V &=7-4 \\
V &=\frac{3}{6}=0.50 \mathrm{~L}
\end{aligned}

Volume of $10 \mathrm{~N} \mathrm{HCI}=0.50 \mathrm{~L}$
Volume of $4 \mathrm{~N} \mathrm{HCl}=1-0.50=0.50 \mathrm{~L}$

\begin{array}{l}
 \Delta \mathrm{T}_{f}=i K_{f} m \\
m=\frac{10 \times 1000}{74.55 \times 100}=1.34 \mathrm{~m} \\
{[\mathrm{Molecular} \mathrm{Mass} \text { of } \mathrm{KCl}=74.55]} \\
\mathrm{i}=\frac{\Delta \mathrm{T}_{f}}{\mathrm{~K}_{f} \mathrm{~m}}=\frac{4.5^{\circ} \mathrm{C}}{\left(1.86^{\circ} \mathrm{C} / \mathrm{m}\right)(1.34 \mathrm{~m})}=1.8
\end{array}

Given, 

\begin{array}{l}
p^{\circ}= \text {vapour pressure of pure solvent}\\
p=\text { vapour pressure of solution } \\
n_{1}=\text { moles of solute } \\
n_{2}=\text { moles of solvent }
\end{array}

Thus, according to Raoult's law.

\frac{p^{\circ}-p}{p^{\circ}}=x_{1}=\frac{n_{1}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}

$\left(x_{1}=\right.$ mole fraction of solute)
or, $\quad 1-\frac{p}{p^{\circ}}=\frac{n_{1}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}$
\frac{p}{p^{\circ}}=1-\frac{n_{1}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}

⇒
\begin{array}{c}
\frac{n_{1}+n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}+n_{2}} \\
\frac{p}{p_{\circ}}=\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}
\end{array}

$\Rightarrow p=p^{\circ} \left( \frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}} \right)$

Given, equal weights of ethane and hydrogen are mixed in an empty container at $25^{\circ} \mathrm{C}$
Initial gram weight $=\begin{array}{ll}\mathrm{C}_{2} \mathrm{H}_{6} & \mathrm{H}_{2} \\ \mathrm{Wg} & \mathrm{Wg}\end{array}
Number of moles $=\frac{w}{30} \quad \frac{w}{2}$ According to Henry's law,

\begin{array}{l}\frac{p_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}}{p_{\text {total }}}=\chi_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}=\frac{n_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}}{n_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}+n_{\mathrm{C}_{2} \mathrm{H}_{6}}}=\frac{\frac{w}{2}}{\frac{w}{2}+\frac{w}{30}} \\ \frac{p_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}}{p_{\text {total }}}=\frac{\frac{w}{2}}{\frac{15 w+w}{30}}=\frac{w}{2} \times \frac{30}{16 w}=\frac{15}{16}\end{array}

So, the fraction of total pressure exerted by
hydrogen is 15: 16

